  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v3.1.7 stable: Two correctness fixes for the seeder peer-wire path + new `TcpListenerService` for inbound TCP peers. (1) **`Wire._message` byte-stream corruption fix** — the old code did `await _push(header); await _push(data);` (two separate writes), which let two concurrent `SendPiece` responses interleave bytes on the underlying transport: `header_A | header_B | data_A | data_B`. Mainline clients (qBittorrent, libtorrent) then failed hash verification on the scrambled blocks and dropped the connection after one good piece. Latent for any seeder that pipelines piece responses to a leecher; never surfaced because forward live-swarm tests had us as the leech, not the seed. Fixed by building the full frame (header + data) into a single buffer and issuing one `_push` call. Plus a `SemaphoreSlim` around `_push` itself for any future paths that send multiple buffers serially. (2) **`TcpPeer.AttachAsync` drop-on-floor race** — when `TcpListenerService` accepted an inbound connection with the BT handshake already kernel-buffered, the old single-method attach started the read loop synchronously inline; `NetworkStream.ReadAsync` resolved immediately and `EmitData` fired before the caller had wired up `OnData -> Wire.DataReceived`. The handshake bytes were dropped. Fixed by splitting into `AttachAsync` (configure socket, fire `EmitConnect`) + `StartReadLoop` (start reading); the listener calls `AddPeer` between them so OnData is wired before any read. (3) **New `TcpListenerService`** — accepts inbound BitTorrent peer-wire connections, peeks the 68-byte BT handshake via `MSG_PEEK` (non-destructive kernel-buffer read), routes by info_hash to the matching torrent in the client, hands the still-unconsumed socket to a responder-mode `TcpPeer`. Closes the last open audit item in `PLAN-BEP52-External-Interop.md` Step 4 (reverse direction: seed-C# / leech-qBittorrent). New `interop_test/qbittorrent_reverse_liveswarm.cs` drives the full live-swarm: SpawnDev.WebTorrent C# seeds, qBittorrent leeches, 1 MiB hybrid torrent SHA-256 byte-identical end-to-end via TCP peer-wire. Both correctness fixes are wire-level and benefit every transport (TCP + WebRTC) — any consumer seeding to a pipelining leecher avoids byte-stream corruption. PlaywrightMultiTest full sweep **950/0/16** in 8m 7s vs 3.1.6 baseline (zero regressions).",
